<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <!--
      Hardened runtime resource access. See README "What a Developer ID build needs".

      TWO THINGS ABOUT THE COMMENTS IN THIS FILE, both learned the hard way:

      AMFI parses this, and its XML reader is stricter than plutil's. A comment may not
      contain a double hyphen (XML forbids it inside a comment, and plutil lets it pass
      while codesign fails with "AMFIUnserializeXML: syntax error"). So codesign flags
      are named here without their leading dashes. Keep it plain ASCII too, for the same
      reason the shaders are: a stray em dash in a comment costs an afternoon.

      Signing without this file, or with a version AMFI cannot read, does not fail
      loudly. The hardened runtime denies these resources BEFORE TCC is consulted, so
      the app installs, launches, shows the prompts, the viewer says yes, and the calls
      fail anyway. Those cues simply do nothing.

      This list is the SHIPPED CUT's needs, not everything the engine can do:

        camera    cue 25's photo booth (photoBooth). The segmenter can also take the
                  camera, but the cut hands it assets/giveit2meclip.mp4 instead.
        location  the probe's personal section (systemProbe, five of them) and cue 14's
                  map, which is authored "here". Without it the probe prints "no fix"
                  and the map falls back to Los Angeles: degradations the piece already
                  handles, but not ones to ship by accident.

      DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and the first one is a trap if the cut changes:

        com.apple.security.automation.apple-events
                  needed the moment a rearrangeIcons or fileSwarm cue is added. Both
                  drive Finder, and while their scripts go out through an osascript
                  subprocess, Permissions.finderAutomationGranted calls
                  AEDeterminePermissionToAutomateTarget IN this process at the gate.
                  Neither cue is in the current timeline. hideOtherApps is NOT Apple
                  Events: it is NSRunningApplication.hide(), which needs nothing.

        com.apple.security.device.audio-input
                  nothing records. The backing track is playback only.

        the App Sandbox file entitlements
                  this app is not sandboxed, so ~/Desktop, ~/Documents and ~/Downloads
                  are pure TCC: the Info.plist usage strings and the viewer's answer are
                  the whole story.

        com.apple.security.personal-information.photos-library
                  the wall reads ~/Pictures as files, never through PhotoKit.
    -->
    <key>com.apple.security.device.camera</key>
    <true/>
    <key>com.apple.security.personal-information.location</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
